Bugs:

  5. Register icon with WM.  I am not motivated to fix this since FLWM
       doesn't display icons like the more popular WMs.  Someone must
       repeatedly nag me to fix it.

  6. FIXED: Codebox::teach()'s adaptive response timeout ("give") had no
       floor.  A run of fast, correct answers shrinks "give" toward the
       student's own average response time, which has natural variance;
       eventually an ordinary timing jitter (not a wrong answer) trips the
       hint/fail path, and no keypress can register in time.  Success turns
       into a timing lottery instead of a knowledge test.  Added MIN_GIVE
       (0.75s default) as a floor in Codebox.cxx.  Tune MIN_GIVE if 0.75s
       is too tight/loose for your own reflexes/keyboard.

  7. FIXED: A "mastered" letter was never actually retired.  Bargraph
       tracks each letter's error estimate as value(), an EMA that only
       ever decays toward zero and never truly reaches it, while
       select()'s weighting is proportional to value() - so a letter the
       student had clearly learned kept a small but nonzero chance of
       being asked again, forever (visually the bar looks completely
       empty well before value() is actually zero: at the Bargraph's
       ~150px slider height, anything below ~0.0067 already rounds to
       under 1px). Added Bargraph::retire(), called from grade() once a
       letter's value() decays below LEARNED_THRESHOLD (Bargraph.cxx):
       the letter is deactivated *and* hidden for good, so select() and
       graduate() both leave it alone permanently. NOTE: LEARNED_THRESHOLD
       is currently set to a fast test value (~5 clean answers) to make
       the retire/complete/reset flow easy to verify; tighten it back
       toward the visually-empty point once confirmed.

  8. ADDED: There was no concept of finishing a lesson - the main loop in
       m.fl is an unconditional while(1), so even after every letter was
       (nominally) mastered, the program just kept asking questions
       forever. With #7 giving "mastered" a real, permanent meaning,
       added Bargraph::complete() (true once every letter has been
       retired) and Bargraph::reset()/Codebox::reset() (undo all
       retire()/graduate()/give-adaptation history). m.fl's main loop now
       checks complete() after each round, shows a completion message,
       and resets the lesson to its first-run state when the student
       clicks OK.

  9. FIXED: Codebox::teach()'s "give" update used the student's raw
       response time (idle_cw()) with no awareness of whether the letter
       just asked was a repeat of the one immediately before it. When a
       weak letter gets drilled several times in a row (very likely,
       since select()'s weighting favors letters with high error), the
       student anticipates it and answers unnaturally fast - and since
       "give" is a single value shared by every letter, that anticipated
       speed was shrinking the response window for every other
       (genuinely unpredictable) letter too. teach() now remembers the
       previously-taught letter (Codebox::last) and skips the "give"
       update whenever the current letter repeats the previous one.

  10. FIXED (crash): Bargraph::retire() (see #7) originally hid a
       mastered letter (clear_visible()) as well as deactivating it, then
       called tesselate() to reflow the remaining bars. tesselate()
       divides by the count of currently-visible sliders
       (Bargraph.cxx: "int dx = w()/nd;"); if the letter being retired
       was the last visible one, that count hit zero and the division by
       zero crashed the app outright (window vanishes instantly - this is
       almost certainly what an earlier tester saw). Fix: retire() no
       longer hides the slider, only deactivates it - mastered letters
       now stay visible on the board (their bar just stays frozen near-
       empty) instead of disappearing, which also removes the crash path
       entirely since visibility, and therefore tesselate()'s divisor,
       never changes because of retirement.

       Leaving mastered letters visible-but-inactive made them look
       identical to letters that simply haven't been introduced yet
       (both are visible + !active()), which would have let
       Bargraph::graduate() and Bargraph::click_letter() mistakenly
       reactivate a mastered letter. Fixed by adding an explicit
       per-letter retired[] flag (separate from active()/visible()) that
       both methods now check, so a retired letter can never be
       reintroduced by any path.

       Also: LEARNED_THRESHOLD raised from a very fast ~5-clean-answer
       test value to ~20 clean back-to-back answers (0.07) - 5 felt like
       letters were just being cycled through rather than genuinely
       drilled.

  11. FIXED: A retired letter's bar never quite reached empty - a thin
       sliver of blue fill remained at the bottom (screenshot confirmed:
       every completed letter showed a small but visible fill after the
       "learned every letter" message). Cause: retire() deactivated the
       Slider but never touched value() - it stayed frozen at whatever
       it was the instant it crossed LEARNED_THRESHOLD (e.g. ~0.06, just
       under the 0.07 cutoff), which the FL_VERT_FILL_SLIDER still
       renders as a small nonzero fill. Fixed by having retire() set
       value(0) explicitly, so a mastered letter's bar always reads
       completely empty.

  12. FIXED: Audio sample rate (Cw.cxx's SDL_AudioSpec) was 11025Hz, a
       holdover from the original 1998-2004 code. At that rate the sine
       wave for a typical tone pitch only gets a handful of samples per
       cycle (~11 at 1kHz, ~6 at the 2kHz top of the Tone knob's range) -
       far too coarse to represent a smooth sinusoid, so the "tone" comes
       out as an audibly rough/distorted buzz rather than a clean note
       (reported on Windows as a "dit" sounding more like a scratchy
       "chvtt"). Likely always somewhat present, just more exposed on a
       modern Windows/SDL2/WASAPI audio path than it apparently was on
       old sound hardware. Raised to 44100Hz (standard modern rate); every
       other computation in Cw.cxx derives from spec.freq dynamically, so
       no other change was needed.

  13. ADDED: There was no volume control - set_cw() always got called with
       loudness=1.0 (full scale), hardcoded in m.fl's adjust() callback.
       An accidental full-volume playback is jarring, and full-scale
       amplitude also leaves no headroom before clipping. Added a third
       "Volume" Knob roller (m.fl, same Speed/Tone pattern, 0..1 range,
       default 0.5) next to Speed/Tone; adjust() now passes Volume->value()
       as loudness. Window/Lesson/Teacher widths grew from 640 to 800 to
       fit the extra control.

  14. FIXED: Lesson->reset() (see #8) unconditionally makes every letter
       (including numbers) visible again and m.fl's completion handler
       re-hides symbols, but it never touched the Morse menu's
       Numbers/Symbols checkboxes - so if a student had unchecked Numbers
       before finishing a letters-only lesson, the reset lesson plan came
       back with numbers included again while the menu still showed
       Numbers unchecked (mismatch between what's actually being taught
       and what the menu claims). Named those two menuitems in m.fl
       (NumbersItem, SymbolsItem) and, in the completion handler, set them
       back to their first-run states (Numbers checked, Symbols
       unchecked) alongside the rest of the reset.

  15. TUNED + CONFIGURABLE: LEARNED_THRESHOLD (see #10) had been raised to
       a felt-right-by-testing 0.07 (~20 clean answers, or so we thought -
       see below) after 0.07's 5-answer predecessor made letters feel like
       they were just being cycled through. In practice this still closed
       letters left-to-right too fast, collapsing the middle stretch where
       several letters are drilled together - by the time a new letter's
       graduate() unblock point (value() < 0.4) was reached, the previous
       letter was already retiring, leaving almost no overlap between
       letters in active rotation.

       Root cause, found by simulating a perfect (never-wrong) student:
       Bargraph::grade()'s "if (overall < 0.1) update(s, pass)" (a
       deliberately-deferred issue, see below) double-applies the EMA for
       any student doing reasonably well, so real decay per clean answer
       is 0.875^2 = 0.765625, not 0.875. Redone against the *actual* decay
       rate, 0.07 was only ~10 clean answers (not ~20), and 0.4's unblock
       point was only ~4 - an overlap window of just 6 answers.

       Replaced the felt-right guess with a value grounded in the original
       complaint that started all of this (letter still asked after its
       bar reads visually empty): the pixel point at which a slider's fill
       rounds to 0px at the default window size (146px-tall bar, 640x240
       window) is value() ~= 0.00342, reached after ~22 clean answers under
       the real (double-EMA) decay - widening the overlap window to ~18
       answers (4 to 22), restoring the middle multi-letter drilling phase
       without changing the eventual left-to-right completion order.

       Also made tunable without a rebuild: LEARNED_THRESHOLD is no longer
       a #define but a static double with a Bargraph::set_learned_threshold()
       setter; m.fl's main() applies argv[1] (if given) to it before the
       lesson starts, e.g. "morse.exe 0.005" to try a different cutoff.

  NOT a code bug: on Windows 11, morse.exe can trigger an "ms-gamingoverlay"
  dialog ("we can't open this link, your device needs a new app") on every
  launch. This is Windows' own Xbox Game Bar auto-detecting a newly
  launched .exe as a possible game and trying to invoke its overlay
  protocol; morse.exe does nothing to request this (FLTK's window is a
  plain Win32 GDI window, no DirectX/fullscreen-exclusive surface). Fix is
  OS-side: right-click morse.exe -> Properties -> Compatibility -> "Disable
  fullscreen optimizations", and/or turn off Xbox Game Bar in Windows
  Settings -> Gaming.

  Known, deliberately deferred (see project history for discussion):
    - Bargraph::grade()'s "if (overall < 0.1) update(s, pass)" applies a
      second error penalty on a FAIL as well as a PASS, so a struggling
      letter's weight can spike late in an otherwise-good session. Left
      alone for now; revisit when tuning how often a hard letter repeats
      across separate teach() calls.
    - Codebox::teach()'s inner while(answer) hint loop is intentionally
      unbounded: a student may want to hear a letter dozens of times to
      internalize it, so it keeps hinting/resounding until answered
      correctly, with no cap. This is deliberate, not a bug.
